@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# AWS-LC integration
+
+AWS-LC is the sole TLS backend. Use the unmodified AWS-LC 5.11.0 dependency
+and shared libraries described in the repository's getting-started guide.
+The extension does not require a vendor-library patch.
+
+TLS operations execute synchronously on the ASTF DP worker that owns the flow.
+Contexts and session state follow worker ownership; no shared crypto worker pool
+or hardware accelerator is required. Full handshakes consume poll-mode CPU time.
+
+See [build dependencies](astf-build-dependencies.md),
+[TLS policy](astf-tls-policy.md) and [validation](astf-tls-validation.md).
diff --git a/doc/astf-cost-metrics.md b/doc/astf-cost-metrics.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-cost-metrics.md
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+# Sampled ASTF/TLS C++ cost metrics
+
+No perf, external process, network proxy or change to TLS/TCP scheduling is
+introduced. TLS-enabled realtime ASTF exposes per-DP counters through
+`get_util_stats()['cpu'][i]['tls_cost']`. The existing text diagnostics runner
+already captures this object in stats.jsonl and stats.csv. Rebuild TRex; no
+profile or Python collector changes are needed.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Set `TREX_ASTF_COST_SHIFT` in the TRex server environment **before startup**:
+
+- unset or `8`: approximately 1/256 calls timed, the default;
+- `10`: approximately 1/1024 calls timed;
+- `off`: disable counts and timing, for an overhead control run;
+- `0`: every call timed, for small functional tests, not a load-test default.
+
+Accepted numeric range is 0..20. Invalid values disable collection and report
+`config_valid=false`; verify `enabled`, `config_valid` and `sample_shift` in
+the collected RPC data. No RPC changes configuration at runtime. This env
+setting is unrelated to sched_max_stretch; preserve the same scheduler
+configuration for comparisons. Both client and server TLS work run here.
+
+## Meaning and scope
+
+Counters live as long as the DP instance, aggregate all profiles on that DP,
+and are not reset by clearing traffic statistics. Use baseline/end deltas.
+Only work inside the realtime ASTF scheduler invocation is bound to counters.
+Profile preparation, SSL_CTX/provider setup, cleanup outside that scheduler,
+and simulator operations without an explicit binding are excluded. Calls
+ending by exception are counted; failed/no-op calls are also included.
+
+`calls` counts completed wrapper invocations, not TLS sessions or records.
+`bytes` counts successful bytes (definition below). These two counters cover
+all observed calls when enabled. A per-category/per-worker xorshift PRNG
+selects timed calls, avoiding deterministic every-Nth-call phase locking.
+No heap allocation, mutex, or locked atomic RMW is used in probes. CP readers
+use independent atomic loads: a concurrent snapshot is not transactional.
+
+Time is `steady_clock_elapsed_ns`, not CPU cycles or thread CPU time. It
+includes preemption, wrapper logic and library-context switching. Network
+waiting between separate calls is excluded. No timestamp is read for an
+unsampled invocation. Probe overhead is nonzero; measure it with an off/on
+comparison before drawing conclusions from small changes.
+
+Fields for each metric:
+
+- `calls`, `bytes`: cumulative totals for all observed calls;
+- `samples`: cumulative timed invocations;
+- `sampled_ns`: sum of their measured elapsed durations;
+- `max_sampled_ns`: lifetime maximum among samples, **not a maximum of all
+  calls**, and not a counter to subtract;
+- `histogram_log2_ns`: sparse cumulative histogram, missing bins mean zero.
+  Bin 0 covers [0,2) ns, bin b=1..30 covers [2^b,2^(b+1)) ns; bin 31 covers
+  [2^31,infinity). Subtract per-bin baselines for interval distributions.
+
+For an interval with unchanged sample_shift and enough samples:
+`mean_ns_per_call ~= delta(sampled_ns)/delta(samples)`;
+`estimated_total_ns ~= delta(sampled_ns) * 2^sample_shift`.
+No samples means unknown, not zero cost. Sampling uncertainty and long-tail
+outliers matter. Maxima and quantiles cannot be reconstructed exactly.
+The sample mean is per **wrapper invocation**, not full-handshake latency.
+Use existing successful handshake counts and resumption counters separately.
+
+## Categories and nesting
+
+| Metric | Measured region / bytes |
+| --- | --- |
+| flow_create_client/server | Flow constructor body: SSL_new, BIO pair, configuration, session lookup; excludes C++ member initialization / outer operator new; bytes=0 |
+| flow_free_client/server | Flow destructor body: session handling, SSL_free, BIO_free; excludes C++ member destruction / outer operator delete; bytes=0 |
+| handshake_client/server | Entire Flow::handshake wrapper, includes SSL_do_handshake, validation and LibraryScope; bytes=0 |
+| read_client/server | Entire Flow::read; bytes = returned plaintext |
+| write_client/server | Entire Flow::write including retry handling; bytes = accepted plaintext |
+| feed_client/server | Ciphertext input into BIO; bytes = accepted ciphertext |
+| shutdown_client/server | Entire Flow::shutdown wrapper; bytes=0 |
+| wire_copy | tls_copy_wire, includes buffer creation, mbuf allocation and memcpy; bytes = ciphertext in successfully completed calls |
+| mbuf_alloc | TLS adapter tls_alloc_wire, includes failed allocation attempts; bytes = requested capacity for successful allocations; not all TRex allocations |
+| app_create | Outer new CEmulAppTls and context lookup; includes flow_create; bytes=0 |
+| app_free | delete CEmulAppTls; includes flow_free and member destruction; bytes=0 |
+| pump | Non-reentrant tls_pump body; includes many of the above; bytes=0 |
+| rx_batch | handle_rx_pkts implementation; includes packet processing, pump, possibly flow creation, TCP and RX/LRO work; bytes=0 |
+
+**Inclusive regions overlap. Never sum all rows as total CPU consumption.**
+In particular, rx_batch/pump, app_create/flow_create, app_free/flow_free and
+wire_copy/mbuf_alloc overlap. Independently sampled parent/child measurements
+are noisy: subtracting them is not exact exclusive timing. The remainder of
+TCP TX, timers and scheduler work is not separately instrumented here.
+BIO output peek/consume, RX reassembly allocations, generic heap allocations
+and memcpy inside OpenSSL do not have standalone counters. These metrics
+localize broad costs; they do not identify individual RSA/AES/malloc internals.
+
+## Measurement procedure
+
+Use the same 32 local DP cores, full-handshake profile, 20000 multiplier and
+300 second duration. Start the rebuilt server normally (default shift=8), then
+run the existing tcp_https_16KB.py text collector. Send its ZIP; no perf.data
+is produced. Check final flow/byte conservation and errors before interpreting
+costs. A second server restart with shift=off and identical traffic gives an
+overhead control. Do not compare different offered loads as an overhead test.
+
+The feature is diagnostic, not a throughput optimization or a guarantee of
+100G. Leave off in routine production runs if instrumentation is not needed.
+Use tests/tls/cost_metrics_test.cpp for correctness and cost_metrics_bench.cpp
+for an isolated probe benchmark. A microbenchmark, especially under emulation,
+does not establish datapath overhead on a physical NIC.
diff --git a/doc/astf-error-drain.md b/doc/astf-error-drain.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-error-drain.md
@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+# HTTP/2 GOAWAY and TLS fatal-alert completion
+
+HTTP/2 clients stop submitting requests after GOAWAY(NO_ERROR), but allow streams accepted by the peer (through last_stream_id) to finish. nghttp2 closes higher stream IDs with REFUSED_STREAM; the adapter preserves remaining accepted responses. Missing or refused requests still make the profile unsuccessful once accepted streams finish. GOAWAY with a nonzero error code remains an immediate error. This behavior applies to cleartext HTTP/2 and HTTP/2 over TLS.
+
+When a failed TLS operation leaves ciphertext in the output BIO, the adapter enters an error-drain phase. It reports the original TLS error once, stops application/handshake timers, discards retained receive buffers and sends already-generated output through the existing bounded TCP/mbuf queue. It does not resume TLS or application processing. TCP acknowledgements release output and resume the drain; after all output is acknowledged the adapter disconnects. A peer TCP reset also ends the flow normally through the existing abort path.
+
+The existing close_timeout_ms bounds error draining, including a peer with a zero TCP window or missing acknowledgements. Allocation failure aborts the drain immediately. No new profile options, counters or dependencies are introduced. close-timeout diagnostics use the existing tcps_tls_close_timeouts counter; the original TLS error is not counted twice. A failed flow cannot publish a resumable session.
+
+Regression coverage:
+
+- tests/tls/run_http2_engine_tests.sh: fragmented/repeated GOAWAY, accepted responses, refused streams, unsubmitted requests, and nonzero error codes, for secure and cleartext configurations.
+- gt_tcp.native_tls_fatal_alert_wire: real TCP adapter, TLS 1.2/1.3, delivery of fatal unrecognized_name, zero-window deadline and exhausted output-mbuf allocator.
+
+The changes are error-path corrections, not throughput optimizations.
diff --git a/doc/astf-handshake-cost.md b/doc/astf-handshake-cost.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-handshake-cost.md
@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# Handshake cost diagnostics, schema 2
+
+AWS-LC implementation. Diagnostic only: no cipher, verification,
+TCP scheduling, resumption or HTTP workload changes. No proxy and no perf.
+Rebuild TRex after applying; C++ Metrics layout changed. Start with
+`TREX_ASTF_COST_SHIFT=8`; `off` disables all probes and negotiation collection.
+The existing text collector preserves the new fields without Python updates.
+
+## RPC fields
+
+`utilization.cpu[i].tls_cost.schema=2`, with `openssl_runtime` from the actual
+linked library. Metric names are serialized as strings. Available metrics:
+
+* `handshake_ssl_client/server`: SSL_do_handshake block including entry-state
+  selection and instrumentation. Does not include result classification.
+* `handshake_result_client/server`: result(rc), including SSL_get_error handling.
+* `handshake_post_client/server`: policy checks, first-success metadata and
+  session candidate handling.
+* `handshake_state_client/server_N`: same SSL call bucketed by state at entry,
+  with `entry_state` human-readable OpenSSL label. 63 aggregates states >=63.
+  One call can traverse several states. This is NOT per-internal-state time and
+  NOT a separate measurement of RSA, X509 or key exchange. Historical labels
+  like SSLv3/TLS do not identify the negotiated protocol; the early-data state
+  label alone does not mean that 0-RTT application data was sent.
+
+All old sampling/histogram semantics apply. Timing is steady-clock elapsed ns,
+not CPU cycles. Outer wrappers include inner probes and instrumentation costs.
+Do not add nested metrics or subtract independent sampled totals as exact
+exclusive time. Clocks are read only for selected invocations, counts always
+update when enabled. New probes and per-success metadata have nonzero overhead;
+use an off control. Metrics memory grows by roughly tens of KiB per worker,
+not per flow. CP reads only atomics or immutable release-published metadata.
+
+`negotiated.client/server` contains the FIRST successful tuple for that side
+in the DP lifetime, `completed` and `different_from_first` counters. Tuple:
+
+* tls_version_id: protocol version (771=1.2, 772=1.3).
+* cipher_protocol_id: wire cipher suite ID.
+* group_id and group_name: OpenSSL negotiated group; group_id may include
+  OpenSSL's unknown-NID flag and must not be blindly decoded as a NID.
+* signature_digest_nid: signature digest NID, NOT signature type.
+* signature_type_nid: key/signature type NID (e.g. rsassaPss).
+
+Client observes the peer server signature. The server's own signature metadata
+is unavailable in this AWS-LC interface and remains zero. Zero or empty also
+means unavailable in some resumed sessions. The pair of signature
+NIDs is not the exact wire SignatureScheme or its PSS salt parameters. If
+`different_from_first` grows, first tuple does not describe the whole workload.
+Counts and tuple publication are safe across threads, but whole snapshots are
+not transactions. As in schema 1, counters cover realtime scheduler-bound work.
+
+## Standalone benchmark
+
+Stop TRex before running this CPU benchmark: even idle poll-mode workers distort
+results. The tool does not stop or signal TRex and refuses to run while a normal
+TRex process is detected. Make sure other CPU-intensive tests are stopped too.
+
+From TRex repository root:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/tls/run_handshake_cost.py \
+  --capture /home/user/https-300s-cpp-cost-32cpu.zip \
+  --output /home/user/https-handshake-cost
+```
+
+Uses the supplied capture only as data, never executes profile.py. Infers pinned
+DP CPU IDs and tcp_https_16KB.py defaults/explicit tunables for cert, key, CA,
+SNI and TLS version. Defaults match the distributed throughput profile; override
+arguments if you edited profile defaults locally. Certificate/key must still
+exist on the server; keys are never copied into output. Encrypted keys are not
+supported and do not trigger an interactive password prompt.
+
+Default thread counts: 1, min(15,N), N, deduplicated. Three repeats, three seconds
+per measurement, deterministic shuffled ordering. `--threads`, `--cpus`,
+`--seconds`, `--repeats`, `--cert`, `--key`, `--ca`, `--hostname`, `--version`
+allow explicit control. On Linux without capture, --cpus is required.
+CXX/CXXFLAGS/LDFLAGS select the toolchain/library. Requires AWS-LC development
+headers/libraries (set CXXFLAGS/LDFLAGS as in astf-tls-validation.md) and a C++11 compiler. Existing engine source is compiled without editing.
+Output is a new directory plus ZIP; failures preserve partial text results.
+
+Full mode uses actual Flow engine and worker-owned Library/Context, certificate
+verification, no resumption, memory BIO transfer and orderly shutdown, no
+application payload. Warmup and context setup are outside the measured loop.
+It measures a different transport/call schedule from NIC traffic. Includes
+Flow creation/destruction, not profile context initialization. Per-worker cost
+counters, first negotiation and runtime OpenSSL version are saved. Full mode
+runs shift=8 and off controls. Result `cps` means completed full sessions per second. Only full exchanges
+are supported. This diagnostic benchmark pins RSA-PSS, X25519 and AES-256-GCM;
+provide an RSA certificate/key. It is not an ECDSA or primitive benchmark.
+
+Binary library paths (ldd on Linux) are compared with captured TRex maps,
+canonicalizing symlinks. A mismatch aborts instead of silently comparing different
+libraries. This is a path check, not proof that files have not changed since the
+capture; output includes current library SHA256 and actual runtime version.
+
+## Tests
+
+`handshake_cost_test.py binary cert key ca`: both TLS versions, exact/sampled/off,
+per-worker lifecycle, state call conservation, negotiation and failed
+hostname verification. `handshake_metadata_test.cpp`: immutable tuple/label
+publication with concurrent reader, mismatch counting and metric names.
+Existing engine/protocol/resumption/worker-context tests must remain green.
diff --git a/doc/astf-http2.md b/doc/astf-http2.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-http2.md
@@ -0,0 +1,230 @@
+# Native HTTP/2 in ASTF
+
+HTTP/2 runs inside each TCP flow on its DP core. TLS is optional: AWS-LC handles encryption when configured; libnghttp2
+handles framing, HPACK, SETTINGS, streams, flow-control windows, PING,
+RST_STREAM and GOAWAY. The codec has no sockets, threads or external processes.
+TLS uses the existing ciphertext queue. Cleartext HTTP/2 writes framed bytes directly to a bounded TCP/DPDK mbuf queue, retaining buffers until ACK.
+Reference: https://nghttp2.org/documentation/programmers-guide.html and RFC 9113.
+
+## Build and example
+
+Install a maintained libnghttp2 development package, including its security
+updates. On Debian/Ubuntu: `apt-get install libnghttp2-dev`. The build checks for
+`nghttp2_option_set_max_continuations`; older unpatched packages without this
+limit are rejected. HTTP/2 is included in --rfc9411 builds, using the same
+AWS-LC prefix. A non-TLS build remains supported and rejects HTTP/2 profiles.
+libnghttp2 is a direct runtime dependency; no nghttpx/nginx/proxy is involved.
+
+Run configure again with your previous options, then rebuild TRex and NIC PMDs.
+For Mellanox, keep the `mlx5-64` target alongside `_t-rex-64`.
+
+```python
+client = ASTFProgram(
+    tls={'sni': 'localhost', 'ca': '/path/ca.pem'},
+    http2={'requests': 10, 'concurrency': 4, 'authority': 'localhost'})
+server = ASTFProgram(side='s',
+    tls={'cert': '/path/server.pem', 'key': '/path/server.key'},
+    http2={'concurrency': 4, 'body_size': 16384})
+```
+
+Use these programs in ordinary ASTF client/server templates. When TLS is configured, ALPN is set to h2; negotiation must actually select h2. TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are supported. Existing
+TLS certificate validation, mTLS and resumption options remain available.
+
+A ready profile is `scripts/astf/https2.py`:
+
+```text
+start -f astf/https2.py -m 1000 -d 60 -t --cert /path/server.pem --key /path/server.key --ca /path/ca.pem --requests 10 --concurrency 4 --body_size 16384
+```
+
+Its MSS defaults to 1440, suitable for IPv6 with MTU 1500 and also usable with
+IPv4. Override using --mss for your topology. The native TRex TCP MSS setting is
+not globally changed by this patch. Certificate paths refer to the TRex server.
+
+## Cleartext HTTP/2 (prior knowledge)
+
+Omit the tls argument on both programs:
+
+```python
+client = ASTFProgram(http2={'requests': 10, 'concurrency': 4})
+server = ASTFProgram(side='s', http2={'concurrency': 4, 'body_size': 16384})
+```
+
+The client sends the HTTP/2 connection preface immediately after TCP setup and
+uses :scheme=http. No TLS context, handshake, encryption or ALPN is used.
+The peer must support prior knowledge; HTTP/1.1 Upgrade is not implemented.
+Client/server roles are serialized by ASTFProgram from side, not user options.
+Existing serialized TLS HTTP/2 profiles without the role field remain accepted.
+
+Ready profile (TCP port 80):
+
+```text
+start -f astf/http2.py -m 1000 -d 60 -t --requests 10 --concurrency 4 --body_size 16384
+```
+
+Use the --rfc9411 AWS-LC/libnghttp2 binary. This is a build
+packaging requirement, not TLS on the wire: cleartext flows leave TLS counters
+at zero. Builds without --rfc9411 still reject HTTP/2 profiles. There is no
+new dependency or configure option in this update.
+
+Cleartext uses the same HTTP/2 counters and connection-level TTFB/TTLB semantics.
+Use separate template groups if encrypted and cleartext flows must be reported
+separately. Session timeout includes TCP establishment for cleartext flows;
+latency samples begin at the initial TCP SYN, including TCP establishment. The deadline
+also bounds draining the output queue. Partial responses and RST fail the flow.
+TX allocation failure aborts one flow and increments tcps_nombuf and
+HTTP/2 error counters; TCP reassembly retains payload for reordered segments.
+
+## Supported profile model
+
+This first native implementation generates GET requests. All requests on a flow
+share the configured authority/path. The server sends a configured status and a
+body consisting of repeated `x` bytes, with Content-Length. Multiple streams
+can be active simultaneously; the client waits for peer SETTINGS and respects
+the smaller of its configured concurrency and the peer's stream limit.
+
+The HTTP/2 program owns the complete exchange. Additional send/recv commands,
+PCAP input, HTTP/1 validation and protocol addons cannot be combined with it.
+A TCP/TLS connection carries the specified number of requests and then closes.
+No replayed HTTP/2 frames are used. Omitting tls selects HTTP/2 prior knowledge over cleartext TCP. Arbitrary POST uploads, custom request headers,
+server push, HTTP/1.1 Upgrade, CONNECT, gRPC and HTTP/3 are not profile features in this
+version. Connection-level failures abort outstanding streams; no automatic
+reconnection or retry of RST_STREAM/GOAWAY-refused requests is performed.
+
+The following http2 keys are supported:
+
+| Key | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---:|---|
+| requests | 10 | Client requests per TCP connection, 1..1000000 |
+| concurrency | 1 | Maximum active streams, 1..128, on each side |
+| authority | localhost | Client :authority, printable ASCII, 1..1024 bytes |
+| path | / | Client :path beginning with /, 1..1024 bytes |
+| body_size | 16384 | Server response bytes, 0..1073741824 |
+| status | 200 | Server final status, 200..599; 204/304 require body_size=0 |
+| expected_status | 200 | Client expected final status, 200..599 |
+| request_interval_ms | 0 | Client pause after each non-final response; 0..3600000, requires concurrency=1 when nonzero |
+| final_hold_ms | 0 | Client hold after the final response before GOAWAY; 0..3600000 |
+| timeout_ms | 30000 | Whole session deadline (after TLS handshake, or at cleartext flow start), 1..600000 |
+| max_body_size | 1073741824 | Maximum received body per stream |
+| max_header_size | 16384 | Maximum decoded header name/value bytes per stream, 512..65536 |
+
+Server-only response options do not configure client requests, and client-only
+request options do not configure the server's reply. The server cannot infer
+how many requests an external client intends to send. Its session deadline
+therefore also bounds otherwise idle sessions.
+
+## Validation, scheduling and resource bounds
+
+libnghttp2 validates HTTP/2 framing and HTTP header semantics, including
+Content-Length when present. A successful client sample requires the configured
+final status and clean END_STREAM; RST_STREAM, incomplete response and protocol
+errors are failures. Bodies without Content-Length are delimited by END_STREAM.
+Body content is not matched against an expected pattern in this version.
+
+One bounded 64-KiB output buffer holds immutable plaintext until AWS-LC accepts
+it. This preserves SSL_write retry arguments even while incoming HTTP/2 control
+frames are processed. DATA reads are capped at 16 KiB irrespective of a peer's
+advertised maximum frame size. Stream slots are allocated once per session;
+response bodies are generated on demand and not stored per flow. libnghttp2
+still performs its own stream/HPACK allocations. Receive header size, continuation
+count, pending control acknowledgements and active streams are bounded.
+
+Work is driven by the existing bounded TLS pump, TCP ACKs, input callbacks and
+DP timer wheel. Session timeout closes a stalled flow. Timer processing can be
+late under overload. HTTP/2 protocol output drains before close_notify; an
+orderly peer close is accepted after all streams finish without requiring a
+GOAWAY acknowledgement. This is not a claim of zero-copy encryption or a
+measured throughput improvement. Physical-NIC performance remains to be tested.
+
+## Statistics
+
+- `tcps_http2_requests`: requests submitted (client) or accepted (server).
+- `tcps_http2_responses`: validated responses (client), serialized responses
+  (server); server completion is not a TCP acknowledgement.
+- `tcps_http2_errors`, `tcps_http2_timeouts`, `tcps_http2_resets`,
+  `tcps_http2_goaway`: protocol/response errors, session timeouts, reset streams,
+  and received GOAWAY frames. Transport/TLS failures also use existing TLS errors.
+- `tcps_http2_ttfb_*`, `tcps_http2_ttlb_*`: client connection latency from TCP SYN.
+
+The origin is the first TCP SYN send attempt; retransmitted SYNs do not reset
+it. This follows the connection-level origin in RFC 9411 section 6.3:
+https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9411.html#section-6.3
+
+TTFB ends at the first input fragment of the first response HEADERS frame.
+The timestamp is retained even if the 9-byte frame header or HPACK block is
+fragmented. TTLB ends when the last response on the connection completes at
+END_STREAM. This covers all requests/parallel streams on the connection and
+includes TCP establishment and, for HTTPS/2, the TLS handshake. SETTINGS,
+WINDOW_UPDATE, PING, GOAWAY, TCP FIN and TLS close_notify do not extend response
+latency. Informational response HEADERS count as the beginning of the response;
+only the final validated status determines success.
+
+Receive timestamps are now captured at TCP input, before reassembly, and travel
+with the retained byte ranges through reassembly and the TLS receive queues.
+Parser/decryption scheduling no longer replaces arrival times. These are software
+TCP-input timestamps, not NIC hardware ingress timestamps: NIC polling and work
+before TCP input remain part of the observation error.
+
+For cleartext, the first response byte uses its segment's timestamp. Completion
+uses the latest arrival among the retained response fragments, so an earlier
+arrival of the final sequence byte cannot hide a delayed missing fragment.
+Duplicate segments do not replace the timestamps of bytes retained by TCP.
+
+TLS precision is **record-level**: authenticated plaintext inherits the first
+wire byte and latest required fragment timestamp of its TLS record. Several HTTP
+frames inside one encrypted record share these bounds; sub-record byte arrival
+cannot be inferred from this interface. The end bound includes the authentication
+tag and any TLS padding. AWS-LC's complete-record callback consumes timestamp
+metadata; successful SSL_read supplies the associated plaintext. Tickets, alerts,
+and empty records consume their own metadata and cannot shift the next record.
+Response samples are still published only for successful complete connections.
+HTTP response frames, rather than HTTP/2 connection-control frames, define the
+response; control data sharing an encrypted record cannot be separated in time.
+
+The same TCP/TLS timestamp transport also applies to verified HTTP/1.1 and its
+transaction counters. No profile option, counter name, SYN origin, sample count,
+or UDP RTT behavior changes. See `astf-rx-timestamps.md` for implementation and
+validation details.
+
+Exactly one sample is committed per successfully completed client connection,
+after clean transport completion. Cached response times are used, so delayed
+cleanup/FIN/close_notify never extends TTLB. Incomplete, invalid, reset or timed
+out connections do not contribute. Their errors remain visible in counters.
+The same samples populate the common tcps_ttfb_* and tcps_ttlb_* aggregates,
+which now include both validated HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 connections. Server latency
+samples remain zero. Template-group aggregation uses the same measurement.
+
+HTTP/2 latency counts measure connections. For N successful connections with 10 responses
+each, tcps_http2_responses is 10*N and each HTTP/2 latency count is N.
+
+Each latency prefix exports avg_ms/min_ms/max_ms/p50_ms/p95_ms/p99_ms, count,
+and bucket_0..bucket_31. Histograms use the existing logarithmic microsecond
+buckets; percentiles are approximate upper bounds capped at the observed max.
+Latency aggregates are absolute for the profile lifetime, like HTTP/1 latency;
+clear_stats reference resets do not subtract them. Ordinary request/error
+counters retain normal TRex counter semantics. Template-group API is supported.
+The server table shows averages/p99 and basic counters; detailed distributions
+remain API-only. Existing HTTP/1/TLS/UDP metrics are renamed without aliases;
+see astf-metric-names.md before updating data collectors.
+
+### Concurrent-connection workloads
+
+Use `request_interval_ms` for sequential request pacing and `final_hold_ms` to
+keep the connection alive after its final response. Both are client-only; the
+server continues to process HTTP/2 control frames normally. Pacing uses the ASTF
+timer wheel without polling or sleeping the DP worker. The absolute `timeout_ms`
+deadline still applies and must include intentional holds plus exchange time.
+It is not extended by progress or by timer wakeups. HTTP response timestamps are
+unchanged: final hold affects connection lifetime, not the last-byte measurement.
+A session completing all responses may close early if its peer sends GOAWAY/EOF;
+these options do not override peer shutdown.
+
+## Mixed response bodies
+
+`http2.body_sizes` is an optional list of 1..128 integer byte lengths. It overrides
+`body_size`; sizes cycle by client stream ID: stream 1 uses element 0, stream 3
+element 1, and so on. Set the same list on both endpoints. This keeps the mapping
+stable under concurrent responses and does not depend on completion order.
+The server generates each selected length; the client checks that exact length
+before counting a successful response. Every length must satisfy `max_body_size`;
+204/304 responses must have zero length. Without a list, fixed-size behavior is
+unchanged. The list is stored in program configuration, without per-request allocation.
diff --git a/doc/astf-metric-names.md b/doc/astf-metric-names.md
