+# Build dependencies
+
+Build with `--rfc9411 --aws-lc-prefix=/absolute/path/to/aws-lc`.
+AWS-LC 5.11.0 is the supported TLS dependency. Install its shared libraries and
+headers under the selected prefix. Native HTTP/2 also requires libnghttp2.
+Follow the repository's getting-started guide for complete configure/build commands.
+
+Use matching headers and runtime libraries. Verify library resolution on the
+machine that will run TRex; do not mix AWS-LC and system libssl objects.
+The OpenSSL command-line utility may be used for test certificate generation;
+it is not a selectable TLS backend for the extension.
diff --git a/doc/astf-rx-timestamps.md b/doc/astf-rx-timestamps.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-rx-timestamps.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# TCP receive timestamps for HTTP latency
+
+Verified HTTP/1.1 and native HTTP/2, encrypted or cleartext, use receive
+timestamps for latency endpoints. Connection TTFB/TTLB start at the initial SYN.
+HTTP/1 transaction latency counters also use the carried receive timestamps.
+
+## Observation boundary and attribution
+
+Only measured flows sample the existing monotonic `now_sec()` clock at TCP input
+for packets with payload. This is before TCP trimming, reassembly and application
+callbacks, but after NIC polling, parsing and flow lookup. This change removes
+subsequent software queue/decryption delays from receive endpoints, not the work
+before the observation boundary. It does not add hardware timestamping or change
+the initial SYN send-attempt origin.
+
+Reassembly payload stores run-length timestamp spans. Merging overlapping blocks
+copies metadata for exactly the suffix whose bytes the existing TCP policy keeps.
+Duplicates cannot overwrite retained timestamps. Byte ranges are delivered with
+their own stamps; the last endpoint is the maximum required arrival time, not
+necessarily the timestamp of the greatest TCP sequence number.
+
+Measured TLS flows retain spans alongside the existing mbuf FIFO, including
+partial BIO writes and delayed pump calls. A second span queue follows bytes fed
+to AWS-LC. The per-SSL record-header callback consumes exactly one full record's
+wire bytes and saves the first-byte and latest-fragment times. Only authenticated
+plaintext returned by SSL_read is attributed to that record. Partial plaintext
+reads retain the same bounds; multiple records in BIO, handshake records and
+post-handshake control records cannot shift attribution. No AWS-LC source patch
+or new dependency is needed.
+
+TLS timing is bounded by records, not exact plaintext-byte-to-packet mapping.
+TTFB uses the first wire byte of the record containing the first response fragment.
+TTLB uses the latest retained fragment of the record containing completion, and
+of earlier response fragments. Authentication tags and padding are included.
+Control and response bytes inside the same encrypted record share these bounds.
+No record is accepted, decrypted or counted before authentication succeeds.
+
+## Resource and compatibility behavior
+
+The mbuf layout, pools and ownership are unchanged. Timestamp queues exist only
+on measured TLS flows and coalesce adjacent equal stamps. Their lifetime follows
+retained ciphertext; storage does not accumulate over the connection lifetime.
+Deque blocks amortize allocations across spans. Reassembly span vectors exist
+only for measured payload. Allocation failure aborts the affected flow through
+existing RESOURCE/HTTP failure handling; no exception escapes a C callback.
+Ordinary unmeasured TLS flows do not install the record callback or create queues.
+No new counters, user knobs, profile changes or UDP changes are introduced.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Synthetic timestamps, including zero and non-monotonic sequence-order arrival.
+- Production TCP overlap merge with duplicates and sequence wraparound.
+- TLS 1.2/1.3 partial record headers, WANT_READ, multiple buffered records, small
+  SSL_read buffers and post-handshake tickets, under ASan/UBSan.
+- HTTP/2 fragmented HEADERS, distinct wire/processing times, parallel streams,
+  late PING/EOF exclusion and independent python-h2 interoperability.
+- TCP/HTTP/TLS simulator regressions with drops, reordering and allocation faults;
+  corresponding build without TLS; DPDK/veth IPv4/IPv6 API validation.
+
+These are local software tests. Physical NIC timestamp precision and throughput
+impact are not measured here. No claim of complete RFC 9411 certification is made.
diff --git a/doc/astf-scheduler-recovery.md b/doc/astf-scheduler-recovery.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-scheduler-recovery.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# ASTF bounded scheduler recovery
+
+The realtime ASTF scheduler now treats the default `sched_max_stretch=0`
+setting as automatic bounded recovery. This applies to ASTF with and without
+TLS. STL and legacy STF retain their original scheduling policy.
+
+Previously, every callback-induced delay beyond the default 100 microsecond
+threshold moved all future deadlines by that delay. Repeated short TLS bursts
+could therefore extend a nominal 300 second run even when CPU capacity was
+available between bursts. This change first processes overdue events against
+the original deadlines. No flow starts, application bytes, or timer events are
+discarded, and no forced stop is introduced at the requested duration.
+
+## Bounds and overload
+
+An episode starts when lateness exceeds the existing stretch threshold. It
+ends when lateness falls back below that threshold. Recovery is allowed for
+less than 10 ms continuously and at most 10 ms of queued time debt. If either
+bound is exceeded, the existing deadline-stretch operation is used. Sustained
+overload can still extend the run; this is intentional, to preserve offered
+work. Normal connection shutdown can also extend the measured duration.
+
+The ASTF work loop returns to its clock/maintenance checks after 32 events or
+100 microseconds of work, whichever is observed first. Checks happen between
+callbacks: a running OpenSSL operation or RX batch cannot be preempted, so
+these are not hard CPU latency guarantees. While overdue, the scheduler also
+services RX when it has not done so for 100 microseconds. This supplements the
+normal queued RX flush event without advancing the TCP timer wheel or changing
+its event ordering. Existing control-plane maintenance remains enabled.
+
+A nonzero explicit `sched_max_stretch` retains its previous stretch-threshold
+meaning (including the existing 100 microsecond minimum). ASTF service checks
+remain active. Remove `--sched-max-stretch-ms` from the diagnostics runner to
+exercise the default policy. Check the saved effective global configuration:
+`sched_max_stretch` must be zero; a value set earlier in an interactive server
+can persist until reset or restart.
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+`ASTFClient.get_util_stats()['cpu'][i]['scheduler']` adds the following fields.
+The existing TLS diagnostics collector, which attaches utilization to its
+`get_stats()` samples, captures them automatically. Bare `get_stats()` does not
+itself fetch utilization.
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `scope` | `dp_lifetime`: shared by all profiles on this DP core |
+| `recoveries` | Recovery episodes entered |
+| `recovered` | Episodes that returned below the stretch threshold |
+| `stretches` | Actual deadline-stretch operations |
+| `stretch_us` | Cumulative deadline extension, in microseconds |
+| `max_lag_us` | Maximum observed positive lateness over the DP lifetime |
+| `waits` | Calls to the scheduler's wait path |
+| `wait_us` | Cumulative time in those waits, in microseconds |
+| `batch_yields` | Work batches ended for an ASTF service check |
+| `rx_polls` | Supplemental RX polls; excludes normal queued RX polls |
+
+Use before/after deltas for cumulative counters. `max_lag_us` is a lifetime
+maximum and must not be subtracted as a duration. Counters are read independently
+and a snapshot is not an atomic transaction. Microsecond conversion truncates
+fractions. Per-core stretch and wait times overlap: their sum is not the run's
+wall-clock extension. Diagnostics remain cumulative across profile stop/start.
+
+For NIC comparison, repeat the same profile, CPU mapping, multiplier and duration
+with default zero stretch, saving the collector archive. Compare wall duration,
+completed CPS, plaintext throughput, retransmits/errors and per-core counter
+deltas. Recoveries with few stretches indicate transient debt was absorbed;
+large stretch deltas indicate debt still exceeds the bounds. Hardware throughput
+and CPU/NUMA scaling require measurement and are not established by simulator
+or virtual-interface tests.
diff --git a/doc/astf-server-sni.md b/doc/astf-server-sni.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-server-sni.md
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# Server-side SNI validation
+
+Native TLS servers check a supplied SNI DNS name against their leaf certificate
+before completing the handshake (RFC 9411 section 4.3.3.1). This applies to TLS
+1.2 and TLS 1.3, HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2. No new profile option is needed.
+
+A mismatch fails the TLS handshake with fatal unrecognized_name; no HTTP
+response is generated. The existing tcps_tls_err_protocol counter accounts for
+this failure. Client verify=False does not disable the server check.
+
+DNS subjectAltName takes precedence over CN. AWS-LC performs case-insensitive
+DNS matching and permits a wildcard only as a complete leftmost label matching
+one label. Partial-label wildcards are rejected. For legacy certificates with
+no DNS SAN, AWS-LC falls back to CN. No new certificate trust policy is imposed
+on the server's own configured certificate. Client chain/hostname validation
+and mTLS remain independent checks.
+
+SNI remains optional: clients without SNI retain their previous behavior.
+When supplied, sni must match the server certificate, including on the backend
+connection opened by a TLS-inspection device. This does not add virtual-host
+certificate selection or an HTTP Host/:authority check.
+
+Run the focused test with the AWS-LC installation used by TRex:
+
+```sh
+CXXFLAGS="-I/opt/trex-aws-lc-5.11.0/include" \
+LDFLAGS="-L/opt/trex-aws-lc-5.11.0/lib -Wl,-rpath,/opt/trex-aws-lc-5.11.0/lib" \
+sh tests/tls/run_server_sni_test.sh
+```
+
+The test creates temporary certificates using the openssl CLI, links the engine
+to AWS-LC, and checks both accepted and rejected handshakes. A permissive client
+isolates server-side rejection; valid names also exchange HTTP response bytes.
