@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+# TLS lifecycle and progress
+
+TLS state is owned by its ASTF flow on one DP worker. The adapter progresses
+handshake, application records and shutdown according to readiness and bounded
+work budgets. Retried writes preserve the pending buffer and length required by
+the TLS library. TCP acknowledgement and application completion are distinct.
+
+See [record scheduling](astf-tls-records.md), [native TLS](astf-native-tls.md)
+and [error draining](astf-error-drain.md). Use the cost diagnostics before
+attributing load limits to a particular TLS operation.
diff --git a/doc/astf-tls-records.md b/doc/astf-tls-records.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-tls-records.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# TLS record-aware receive scheduling
+
+Record scheduling preserves certificate verification,
+full handshakes, TLS record contents and HTTP transaction boundaries. No new
+profile options, resumption shortcuts, crypto workers or library changes.
+
+## Receive
+
+The flow tracks the five-byte record header and skips over its payload length
+as bytes enter the bounded BIO. This is a scheduling hint, not a TLS validator.
+After SSL_read returns WANT_READ, fragments of an incomplete record no longer
+wake it. A complete record or a full BIO wakes it. Invalid record headers
+restore eager processing so OpenSSL/AWS-LC sees the header immediately.
+The library decides whether the error is diagnosed at the header or body.
+EOF, write dependencies and output release retain their existing wakeups.
+Buffered plaintext remains readable without new network input.
+
+ASTF enables this only when the TCP receive high-water mark can hold the maximum
+supported ciphertext record (18432+5 bytes). Smaller windows use partial reads
+so that credit is returned before the complete record arrives. Disabling
+coalescing explicitly releases an existing WANT_READ wait. BIO capacity pressure
+also releases it, including the 128-byte transport used by regression tests.
+The parser observes the stream even when coalescing is disabled.
+
+The pump feeds up to 16 queued mbuf segments before the next SSL operation,
+within its existing bounded outer budget. It stops feeding when BIO is full.
+No per-packet heap allocations or new RX mbuf retention queue are introduced.
+The existing TCP window continues to count bytes retained in the input BIO.
+
+## Transmit
+
+Each fixed ciphertext slot now has nine 2-KiB mbuf descriptors, allowing a
+16-KiB application record plus AEAD overhead to fit in one slot. Previously
+that record could be split into a 16384-byte slot and a tiny tag/tail slot.
+The 64-KiB ciphertext queue bound, eight slots, ACK-based lifetime and retransmit
+ownership remain in place. This costs eight extra CMbufObject descriptors per
+flow (128 bytes on the tested 64-bit layout). It reduces queue bookkeeping;
+it does not eliminate encryption copies or guarantee fewer mbuf allocations.
+
+We retain 2-KiB allocation: the existing 4-KiB pool has an explicit exhaustion
+fallback in TRex. CMbufBuffer already uses fixed descriptors in this path;
+Create resets them and is not itself a vector allocation here.
+
+## Certificate work
+
+A local diagnostic, tests/tls/handshake_verify_cost.cpp, times the default
+X509_verify_cert call through the library's certificate-verification callback.
+It preserves the existing store, hostname parameters and verification policy.
+It does not bypass validation or cache an accepted certificate.
+
+The diagnostic measures full handshakes only; it is not an HTTPS throughput
+benchmark and excludes application traffic. ARM timing cannot establish the
+cost breakdown on the user's Xeon. Its result did not justify adding a complex
+validation-result cache. DER parsing, CertificateVerify and key agreement are
+outside this X509_verify_cert measurement. No certificate cache is used.
+
+## Tests
+
+run_engine_test.sh includes record_coalescing_test.cpp. Engine and independent
+peer protocol tests enable coalescing: TLS 1.2/1.3, malformed/truncated records,
+KeyUpdate, small BIO, WANT_WRITE, pending plaintext and shutdown. The new test
+compares enabled/disabled scheduling with 1-byte and 1460-byte input fragments;
+it also checks disabling coalescing mid-record and immediate invalid-length
+dispatch to the TLS library. The optional third argument to its executable measures only the
+post-handshake data path (100 alternating runs, 164784 plaintext bytes).
+
+The simulator tests exercise small TCP windows, retransmissions, reassembly,
+resource exhaustion, deadlines and cleanup. Live DPDK/veth validates application
+lifecycle; physical NIC throughput remains to be measured. Do not convert the
+reduction in SSL_read calls directly into a claimed total CPU reduction.
diff --git a/doc/astf-tls-runtime.md b/doc/astf-tls-runtime.md
