+++ b/doc/astf-tls-runtime.md
@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+# TLS runtime scope
+
+The extension uses AWS-LC in the native TCP/ASTF/DPDK execution path. It supports
+TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, certificate verification, mutual TLS and session resumption.
+The profile selects TLS policy explicitly; see [policy](astf-tls-policy.md).
+
+A TLS-capable engine is only one component of an RFC 9411 test. The operator must
+select and report workload, negotiated algorithms, authentication, session reuse,
+DUT inspection settings and measurement conditions. Tests must distinguish full
+handshakes from resumed sessions. No certification or hardware throughput claim
+is implied by enabling the extension.
+
+Use the repository's RFC 9411 scope and limitations documents for the complete
+benchmark boundary, and [validation](astf-tls-validation.md) for local tests.
diff --git a/doc/astf-tls-policy.md b/doc/astf-tls-policy.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-tls-policy.md
@@ -0,0 +1,153 @@
+# TLS policy and mutual authentication
+
+AWS-LC is the TLS backend. TLS is opt-in per ASTF program.
+
+## TLS policy
+
+ASTFProgram(tls={...}) accepts these additional policy fields on both endpoints:
+
+* `cipher_suite`: one suite for the configured `version` (no colon expressions).
+  TLS 1.2 accepts ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256,
+  ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256, ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384,
+  ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384, ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305,
+  ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305. TLS 1.3 accepts TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,
+  TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 or TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256.
+* `groups`: nonempty ordered list from X25519, P-256, P-384, P-521.
+* `signature_algorithms`: nonempty ordered list from rsa_pss_rsae_sha256/384/512,
+  ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256, ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384, ecdsa_secp521r1_sha512,
+  ed25519, rsa_pkcs1_sha256/384/512. The slash notation here abbreviates three
+  separate names. Profiles use full names, e.g. `rsa_pss_rsae_sha256`.
+
+Omitted fields keep existing defaults. Unknown names, duplicate/empty lists,
+wrong types and a cipher for the other TLS version are rejected before traffic.
+The library negotiates the intersection with the peer: incompatible certificate,
+group or signature policies fail the handshake; there is no silent fallback
+outside the configured policy. RSA PKCS#1 signatures cannot sign a TLS 1.3
+CertificateVerify. Certificate-chain signatures and handshake signatures are
+related but distinct; a restrictive list must also be compatible with the chain.
+TLS version remains an exact choice, not a range. Profiles are immutable after
+loading and worker contexts inherit all policy fields.
+
+## mTLS
+
+Client: `tls={'cert':'/path/client.pem','key':'/path/client.key',
+'ca':'/path/server-ca.pem','sni':'service.example'}`.
+Server: `tls={'cert':'/path/server.pem','key':'/path/server.key',
+'require_client_cert':True,'ca':'/path/client-ca.pem'}` on `side='s'`.
+
+All paths are on the TRex server. Certificates may include their intermediate
+chain. The server CA file supplies trust anchors and advertised acceptable CA
+names. `require_client_cert` requires explicit CA and verification enabled.
+Absent, expired, untrusted and inappropriate-EKU client certificates fail the
+handshake. The client still verifies the server chain and hostname independently.
+Trust authenticates a client certificate; no application authorization mapping,
+CRL/OCSP fetching, post-handshake client authentication or identity ACL is added.
+Session resumption is optional and preserves the authenticated session; it does
+not repeat certificate validation on each resumed connection. Reloading a profile
+creates new ticket contexts. Full-handshake benchmarks keep `resumption=False`.
+
+## HTTP validation and latency
+
+Opt in on the TCP client: `ASTFProgram(http={'responses':10})`.
+For HTTPS add `tls=...`; ALPN must be `http/1.1`. Ordinary HTTP works both
+with and without a `--rfc9411` build. UDP, server-side response validation
+and addon programs are rejected. The response validator is for HTTP/1.x GET workloads.
+ASTF send/recv commands still drive the exchange; `responses` must match the
+number of responses expected on that connection, including profile loops.
+
+HTTP options (all integers, booleans rejected):
+
+| Option | Default | Allowed |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| responses | 1 | 1..1000000 |
+| expected_status | 200 | 200..599 |
+| max_header_bytes | 16384 | 512..65536 |
+| max_body_bytes | 1073741824 | 0..1073741824 |
+
+The incremental parser consumes authenticated plaintext, without copying body
+bytes. It handles fragmented headers/body, multiple responses, informational
+responses, Content-Length, chunked framing/trailers and close-delimited bodies.
+A close-delimited body completes on peer FIN for plain TCP, or authenticated
+TLS close_notify for HTTPS. Reset and timeout never supply a valid EOF.
+Conflicting/duplicate framing headers, excess responses, malformed headers,
+limits, unexpected status or incomplete EOF abort that flow. Header and trailer
+budgets are bounded separately per response; chunk-size lines are bounded too.
+HEAD, CONNECT, upgrades, other transfer codings and HTTP/2 are not supported by
+this validator. Body content/digest is not checked. Without `http`, arbitrary
+TCP/TLS byte programs behave as before and produce no HTTP latency samples.
+
+Client statistics exposed through the existing ASTF stats API/console:
+
+* `tcps_http_responses`: complete responses with expected status (transaction count).
+* `tcps_http_errors`, `tcps_http_bad_status`, `tcps_http_truncated`,
+  `tcps_http_framing`: validation failures and reason counters.
+* `tcps_http_aborted`: flows without validation error that nevertheless failed
+  to complete validated HTTP plus clean stream close (reset, timeout, stop, etc.).
+* `tcps_ttfb_avg_ms/min_ms/max_ms`, `tcps_ttlb_avg_ms/min_ms/max_ms` and
+  `tcps_ttfb_count`, `tcps_ttlb_count`: successful connection latency samples.
+
+One latency sample per connection, published when the flow is destroyed, only
+if all expected responses and a clean stream close succeeded (peer FIN without
+socket error or local abort for TCP; also bidirectional close_notify for TLS). TTFB is
+first HTTP plaintext delivery minus first SYN enqueue time; TTLB
+uses last HTTP plaintext delivery. Retransmitted SYNs do not restart the clock.
+TLS handshake/tickets/alerts are excluded from the data endpoint. TCP FIN wait
+is excluded from the measured interval. Failed/partial connections have no
+successful latency samples; completed-response counts may still include valid
+responses preceding a later failure. Never divide latency sums by transactions.
+
+Aggregation across workers/template groups uses weighted averages and true
+min/max. A carry word protects nanosecond sums from 64-bit overflow. These are
+software timestamps, including local polling and TLS processing latency, not
+NIC wire timestamps. Clear/reload resets follow existing ASTF statistics scope;
+latency fields are absolute gauges, not client-side delta counters.
+
+The origin follows RFC 9411 section 6.3 (first SYN), not per-request send time.
+The successful-only sampling policy, authenticated-plaintext endpoint and the
+lack of hardware timestamps must be stated in reports. Adding these capabilities
+does not establish whole-generator RFC 9411 compliance. Benchmark methodology,
+traffic mix, cipher/key requirements, HTTP/2 and DUT validation remain separate.
+Reference: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9411.html#section-6.3
+
+The supplied astf-ttfb-ttlb.patch was reviewed and partly reused: latency
+accumulators, CP gauges and cross-worker merge integration. Its raw TCP receive
+hooks were replaced because a TLS handshake record is not HTTP data, and its
+unconditional close sampling could count incomplete responses. New tests cover
+the actual decrypted flow path, not just extracted stubs.
+
+## Example and cost
+
+`scripts/astf/https_verified.py` provides a 16 KiB response, ten GET exchanges per
+connection by default; `--responses` and `--body-size` change the workload.
+Pass `--client-cert`, `--client-key`, `--client-ca` together to enable mTLS.
+Example after generating suitable certificates:
+
+```
+start -f astf/https_verified.py -m 1000 -d 60 -t --cert /tmp/server.pem --key /tmp/server.key --ca /tmp/ca.pem --sni localhost --version 1.2 --cipher-suite ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256 --groups P-256 --signature-algorithms ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 --client-cert /tmp/client.pem --client-key /tmp/client.key --client-ca /tmp/ca.pem
+```
+
+The parser and timing state belong to the ASTF application, independently of TLS.
+In-order plain TCP mbuf chains are parsed before release, without another copy.
+Out-of-order bytes are retained only for TLS or opted-in HTTP flows; delivered
+plain reassembly vectors are parsed directly without allocating more mbufs.
+Unvalidated TCP retains its byte-count-only reassembly behavior. Each reassembly
+block includes a shared-pointer slot in all builds. Allocation failure for a
+validated flow clears its reassembly queue and aborts that flow.
+
+Plain HTTP example: `start -f astf/http_verified.py -m 1000 -d 60`.
+It defaults to ten 16-KiB responses per connection. Use `-t --responses 1
+--body-size 1024` to change the workload. HTTPS uses `https_verified.py` below.
+HTTP validation is opt-in in existing profiles: add `http={'responses': N}` to
+the client program and set N to the number of expected GET responses.
+
+HTTP parsing adds bounded CPU work and one header buffer allocation per enabled
+flow (default 16 KiB plus parser metadata). Set an appropriate header bound for
+the traffic if memory matters. It is not allocation-free. mTLS adds certificate
+bytes, signing and verification; compare its performance separately from server-
+only authentication. No NIC throughput claim is made.
+
+Tests: `sh tests/tls/run_features_test.sh`, `sh tests/tls/run_engine_test.sh`,
+`python3 tests/tls/api_test.py` with the usual client PYTHONPATH, simulator
+`gt_tcp.native_tls_http_validation`, and `run_interactive_veth.sh` now exercise
+mTLS, explicit policy and HTTP latency on the completed HTTPS stage, plus
+ordinary HTTP latency via the same ASTF stats API on IPv4 and IPv6.
diff --git a/doc/astf-http-validation.md b/doc/astf-http-validation.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-http-validation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+# HTTP response content, request latency and negotiated TLS
+
+HTTP validation and measurements work with and without TLS. Negotiated
+parameter metadata applies to TLS connections.
+
+## Streamed body validation
+
+```python
+client = ASTFProgram(http={
+    'responses': 10,
+    'expected_status': 200,
+    'body_contains': 'unique expected origin content',
+    'transactions': True,
+})
+```
+
+For HTTPS also pass the existing `tls` options. `body_contains` is a nonempty
+UTF-8 string of at most 4096 encoded bytes. The exact bytes must occur at least
+once in **each** response body. Matching is case sensitive. It operates on the
+body after HTTP chunk framing removal, not on headers, chunk extensions or
+trailers. It handles boundaries between mbufs, TLS records and HTTP chunks.
+The match resets for every response. Bodies are never retained. A bounded KMP
+matcher prevents quadratic work on repetitive adversarial input and stops
+scanning after the first match. Omit the option to retain framing-only checks.
+
+An absent match at a complete response boundary increments `tcps_http_bad_content`
+and `tcps_http_errors`, aborts the flow and excludes that response from successful
+response/transaction counters. A truncated response remains a truncation error.
+A block page with HTTP 200 is therefore rejected if the expected marker is absent.
+This is substring validation, **not SHA-256 or exact body equality**. Content-Encoding
+is not decoded: request identity encoding if the marker describes uncompressed text.
+HEAD, CONNECT, protocol upgrades and HTTP/2 remain outside the GET validator.
+
+## Per-request measurements
+
+Enable `transactions=True` and mark each logical request before sending it:
+
+```python
+client.set_var('n', 10)
+client.set_label('request')
+client.http_request_start()
+client.send(request_part_1)
+client.send(request_part_2)
+client.recv(response_wire_length)
+client.jmp_nz('n', 'request')
+```
+
+`http_request_start()` waits internally for TCP establishment if needed, then
+records the measurement marker. It may be used inside profile loops.
+For TLS the application starts after the TLS handshake. The origin is execution
+of the marker immediately before the request send commands, **not a NIC wire
+transmit timestamp**. It includes subsequent local scheduling/encryption/TCP
+backpressure. Do not insert unrelated delays between the marker and sends.
+One marker covers a request split across any number of send buffers. Automatic
+inference from send boundaries would miscount those requests and is not used.
+
+Responses map to markers FIFO, as required by HTTP/1.x ordered responses. At most
+64 outstanding markers are supported. A response without a marker, duplicate
+response start, overflow, or a marker without the feature enabled is rejected;
+runtime failures increment `tcps_http_transaction_errors`. Pending markers at
+flow destruction prevent a successful connection sample. Loops must mark every
+iteration. A marker is a profile contract: this feature does not parse or validate
+outbound requests. With `transactions=True` but no markers, a response is an error.
+
+* `tcps_http_req_ttfb_*`: marker to first response byte, including informational 1xx.
+* `tcps_http_req_ttlb_*`: marker to end of the complete response (including framing and
+  trailers). A close-delimited response validates at EOF but uses the timestamp
+  of its last data byte, excluding the FIN/close_notify wait.
+* Each prefix exports `count`, `avg_ms`, `min_ms`, `max_ms`, `p50_ms`,
+  `p95_ms`, `p99_ms`, plus `bucket_0` through `bucket_31`.
+
+Each **validated response** commits one pair of samples immediately. Later failure
+of another response or transport does not remove earlier successful transaction
+samples. This differs intentionally from the existing connection `tcps_ttfb_*`/`tcps_ttlb_*`
+counters, which still publish one successful sample at flow destruction starting
+at the first SYN. The two namespaces must not be compared as identical metrics.
+
+Histogram buckets are disjoint, not cumulative: bucket 0 is [0,1] microseconds;
+bucket i (1..30) is (2^(i-1),2^i] microseconds; bucket 31 is all values above 2^30
+microseconds. Latency sums/min/max are measured in nanoseconds. Percentiles use
+nearest-rank bucket **upper-bound estimates**, capped at the observed maximum;
+they are approximate, potentially up to a factor of two above the true quantile.
+Min/avg/max are not rounded to histogram buckets. Merge sums histogram counts and
+uses weighted averages and true min/max across workers/template groups. Counts
+and histogram bins are absolute gauges, following existing connection-latency
+reset semantics. All fields are available through the existing ASTF stats API.
+
+For pipelined/coalesced replies in one TCP delivery or decrypted buffer, several
+boundaries can share a software timestamp. No per-byte hardware time is implied.
+
+## Actual TLS parameters
+
+A completed handshake records exactly one entry in each bounded counter family,
+on each endpoint, from the AWS-LC connection (never inferred from configuration):
+
+* `tcps_tls_version_tls12`, `tcps_tls_version_tls13`, `tcps_tls_version_other`.
+* `tcps_tls_cipher_<IANA/OpenSSL-style name>` and `tcps_tls_cipher_other` (hyphens become
+  underscores, e.g. `tcps_tls_cipher_ecdhe_ecdsa_aes128_gcm_sha256`).
+* `tcps_tls_group_none`, `tcps_tls_group_x25519`, `tcps_tls_group_p256`, `tcps_tls_group_p384`,
+  `tcps_tls_group_p521`, `tcps_tls_group_other`.
+* `tcps_tls_peer_signature_<scheme>`, `tcps_tls_peer_signature_none`,
+  `tcps_tls_peer_signature_other`.
+* `tcps_tls_full_handshakes` and existing `tcps_tls_resumed`; their sum equals
+  `tcps_tls_handshakes`.
+
+The signature describes **peer handshake authentication**, not the certificate
+issuer's signature or this endpoint's own outgoing signature. On the client it
+identifies server handshake authentication (the inspecting NGFW when present).
+On the server it describes client authentication with mTLS; without client
+authentication `none` is expected. Resumed handshakes have no new authentication
+signature and explicitly report `none` even if the library retains session metadata.
+TLS 1.2 resumed handshakes also report group `none` because there is no new key
+exchange; TLS 1.3 reports the negotiated group. Unknown IDs go into `other` rather
+than being mislabeled. Counts are per handshake, not per record/request, and failed
+handshakes do not contribute. Existing TLS error counters remain available.
+
+## Examples, tests and overhead
+
+`astf/http_verified.py` and `astf/https_verified.py` now mark each request loop
+and enable transaction measurements. Nonempty generated bodies are checked for
+`x`; use a distinctive application marker for a real DUT test.
+
+Validation: standalone HTTP matcher/queue/histogram tests, TLS 1.2/1.3 policy+mTLS
+metadata checks, TCP loss/reordering and bad content tests, and a live ASTF RPC
+lifecycle run on IPv4/IPv6 with three requests per connection. The live run also
+rejects a substituted HTTP 200 body and verifies histogram/sample counts.
+
+The enabled matcher adds pattern/prefix storage per HTTP flow. The transaction
+FIFO adds one bounded allocation only when enabled. Histograms add fixed storage
+to TCP statistics per worker/template group (two 32-bin histograms, including
+summaries), not per flow. No per-response allocation or dynamic TLS parameter
+map is used. These features add work; no new physical-NIC performance claim is made.
