@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# Native TLS validation
+
+The production backend is AWS-LC only. OpenSSL CLI remains useful for generating
+fixtures and as an independent interoperability peer; it is not a selectable
+production backend. External PSK profiles are rejected; certificate-based ticket
+resumption remains supported. HTTP/2 additionally requires maintained nghttp2.
+
+From the source root, after installing AWS-LC shared libraries:
+
+```sh
+export AWS_LC_PREFIX=/opt/aws-lc
+# Use lib64 instead if that is the actual installation directory.
+export CXXFLAGS="-I$AWS_LC_PREFIX/include"
+export LDFLAGS="-L$AWS_LC_PREFIX/lib -Wl,-rpath,$AWS_LC_PREFIX/lib"
+sh tests/tls/run_engine_test.sh
+sh tests/tls/run_http2_engine_tests.sh
+python3 tests/tls/build_prefix_test.py
+# Requires the AWS-LC-enabled ASTF simulator built by linux/b.
+sh tests/tls/run_profiles.sh
+```
+
+`run_engine_test.sh` includes error-classification regressions and a 16-seed
+record-fuzzer smoke test. Verification failures, invalid records and EOF are
+separate outcomes. EOF without close_notify is truncation, even before a
+certificate exists. Remote alerts are not local certificate verification errors.
+
+For coverage-guided testing, use `tests/tls/record_fuzzer.cpp` with libFuzzer
+and AWS-LC. Supply certificate fixtures through `TREX_TLS_TEST_CERT` and
+`TREX_TLS_TEST_KEY`, and compile without NDEBUG to keep authentication assertions
+active. Instrument AWS-LC separately for coverage inside the crypto library.
+
+Handshake cost validation:
+
+```sh
+c++ -std=c++11 -O2 -DTREX_TLS -pthread $CXXFLAGS -Isrc \
+  tests/tls/handshake_bench.cpp src/44bsd/tls/astf_tls.cpp \
+  $LDFLAGS -lssl -lcrypto -o /tmp/trex-handshake-bench
+python3 tests/tls/handshake_cost_test.py /tmp/trex-handshake-bench cert.pem key.pem cert.pem
+```
+
+Use an RSA localhost certificate for this pinned RSA benchmark.
+`run_handshake_cost.py` schedules supported full handshake exchanges.
+
+Server own-signature diagnostic fields are unavailable and remain zero.
+Client TLS parameter counters record the negotiated peer signature.
+Cost diagnostics are optional and add measurement overhead.
diff --git a/doc/astf-udp-rtt.md b/doc/astf-udp-rtt.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/astf-udp-rtt.md
@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# ASTF UDP transaction RTT
+
+Opt-in API for sequential, single-datagram request/response exchanges:
+
+```python
+c = ASTFProgram(stream=False)
+c.udp_request(b'hello', timeout_ms=1000)
+s = ASTFProgram(stream=False)
+s.recv_msg(1)
+s.send_msg(b'world')
+```
+
+`udp_request(buf, timeout_ms=1000, size=0, fill=None)` replaces BOTH
+`send_msg(buf)` and its matching `recv_msg(1)`. It sends one datagram and blocks
+until the next datagram received on this flow or a deadline expires. Put it in
+an ASTF loop to measure every exchange. Existing `send_msg`/`recv_msg` retain
+their behavior and collect no RTT samples. TCP TTFB/TTLB are unchanged.
+
+Only a UDP client template without an addon is supported. The matched response
+is consumed by udp_request, independently of recv_msg's cumulative watermarks.
+The peer uses ordinary recv_msg/send_msg. There are no timestamps, IDs or extra
+headers on the wire; profile payloads and packet lengths remain unchanged.
+
+## Measurement and failure semantics
+
+The clock starts just before the application's send call and stops in the UDP
+receive callback. This is software request-response time including TRex TX
+buffering, network/DUT in both directions, peer application delay, and polling
+latency. It is not NIC hardware timestamping or pure DUT latency. Server delay
+is intentionally included. No per-request allocation or payload copy is added;
+two uint64_t fields are added to CEmulApp, and summaries are per profile/group.
+
+The timeout is an integer 1..60000 ms (default 1000). At the deadline the flow
+closes; subsequent commands on that flow are not run. The receive callback also
+checks the deadline, so a delayed timer does not admit an expired response.
+The ASTF timer wheel handles idle timeout processing; under overload actual
+cleanup may be later than the deadline. Generic UDP keepalive does not preempt
+a pending measured request. A stop/other closure before completion counts as
+aborted, not a successful response. Only completed transactions enter the
+histogram. Missing answers must be reported alongside latency percentiles.
+
+This command assumes one outstanding request and one response datagram. It does
+not parse the application protocol or validate contents, sequence IDs, duplicates
+or multi-datagram responses. A duplicated response received while the next request
+is pending can be attributed to that next request. Closing on timeout prevents
+advancing to another request on that same flow, but does not solve stale datagrams
+after tuple reuse. Use protocol-specific correlation for such scenarios. No UDP
+retransmission, jitter or one-way latency is implemented by this feature.
+
+## API and screen
+
+`client.get_stats(skip_zero=False)['traffic']['client']` exposes:
+
+- `udps_rtt_avg_ms`, `udps_rtt_min_ms`, `udps_rtt_max_ms`;
+- `udps_rtt_p50_ms`, `udps_rtt_p95_ms`, `udps_rtt_p99_ms`;
+- `udps_rtt_count` — completed exchanges;
+- `udps_rtt_bucket_0` ... `udps_rtt_bucket_31` — disjoint sample counts;
+- `udps_rtt_started`, `udps_rtt_timeouts`, `udps_rtt_aborted`.
+
+For a named profile pass `pid_input=...`. With `tg_name='udp_rtt'`, use
+`client.get_traffic_tg_stats('udp_rtt', skip_zero=False, pid_input=...)` for the
+same group counters. Counters on the server side remain zero for this feature.
+
+All these metrics are absolute profile-lifetime aggregates, like existing HTTP
+latency statistics. API clear_stats reference resets do not subtract them.
+Reload/restart the profile for a fresh measurement interval. At flow completion:
+started = count + timeouts + aborted. During a test the difference is the
+number of pending measured requests. Read counters after traffic stops for a
+stable identity; live cross-core snapshots are not atomic.
+
+Bucket 0 covers 0..1 us; buckets 1..30 cover (2^(i-1),2^i] us; bucket 31 is the
+remaining overflow. Percentiles are approximate upper bucket bounds, capped at
+the observed maximum, not exact percentiles. Aggregation merges sample counts,
+sums with carry, and true min/max across workers and template groups.
+
+Only avg/p99, started and nonzero timeout/abort counters are shown on the server
+screen. Histogram, sample count and other summaries remain available through
+API. For zero-valued summaries use skip_zero=False.
+
+## Example
+
+`scripts/astf/udp_imix_rtt.py` adapts the supplied udp_imix.py. Defaults retain
+64-byte untagged IPv4 frames (including FCS), one request/response per flow.
+`--frame_size_b`, `--flow_size`, `--delay`, `--ramp_up` are preserved; new option
+`--timeout_ms` selects the response deadline. flow_size must be even and >=2;
+frame_size_b must be 64..1518 (IPv4, MTU 1500). The profile still uses one fixed
+frame size per invocation, not a distribution of IMIX sizes. It keeps the
+original arbitrary x/y payloads and UDP port 53; it does not implement DNS.
+
+```text
+start -f astf/udp_imix_rtt.py -m 1000 -d 60 -t --frame_size_b 64 --flow_size 20 --timeout_ms 1000
+```
+
+## Tests
+
+- `tests/tls/udp_rtt_api_test.py`: serialization, legacy behavior, invalid options,
+  unsupported server template.
+- `gt_tcp.udps_rtt_merge`: true worker min/max, histogram totals, sum carry.
+- `tests/tls/run_udp_rtt_veth.sh`: dedicated NET_ADMIN Linux container only;
+  real ASTF RPC/DPDK, IPv4 and optional `--ipv6`, repeated requests, server delay,
+  timeout exceeding keepalive, late answer, forced stop, profile reload, TG stats,
+  clear_stats semantics, no TCP latency updates, unchanged L7 byte counts.
